      "preview_head": "s sewage or other waste effluents. In particular, the invention provides a waste treatment process and apparatus in which a high degree of separation is initially provided for separating solid and liq",
      "preview_tail": "d Description The first step in this procedure requires passing the waste effluent through two or more filters with differing pore sizes to remove larger solid components, such as clay, gravel, and ot",
      "full_text": "s sewage or other waste effluents. In particular, the invention provides a waste treatment process and apparatus in which a high degree of separation is initially provided for separating solid and liquid components, with the solid component thereafter incinerated with little or no resulting solid waste product remnants. The method and apparatus also includes an optional system for treatment of the gaseous products produced during the procedure. 2. Background Art Traditional techniques for treating groundwater and other kinds of sewage generally include a mix of physical, chemical, physiological systems to eliminate particles from groundwater before it is transferred back into natural bodies or used in other systems. These treatments can involve sedimentation tanks where heavy ions settle at the bottom; filter devices that trap suspended solids through porous media; and activated sewage processing which utilizes chemicals to break down organic matter. Unfortunately, these conventional techniques would not be adequate for treating many kinds of waste effluents or maintaining low levels of separation between solid and liquid materials. However, they also create large quantities of excess solid wastes that require further handling and disposal, leading to environmental problems if properly managed. 3. Summary The present invention addresses the difficulties identified with traditional sewage treatment technologies by introducing a novel procedure for treating materials such as water or other waste effluents which ensures an optimal separation between solids and fluids in addition to removing solid waste products post-treatment through disposal, thereby avoiding ecological impact. The approach comprises of: - A primary phase where the water is subjected to a high-efficiency filter system that separates out large molecules from liquid components. - An optional secondary treatment process which can involve chemical or biological treatments for further purification and removal of dissolved substances. 4. Detailed Description The first step in this procedure requires passing the waste effluent through two or more filters with differing pore sizes to remove larger solid components, such as clay, gravel, and ot",
